                'War at sea: Britain imposed a blockade on Germany from 1914. Jutland (31 May-1 June 1916) was the only major fleet battle: Britain lost 14 ships and about 6,000 men, Germany 11 ships and about 2,500 men, but the German High Seas Fleet never seriously challenged the Royal Navy again - a tactical German success but a strategic British victory.',
                'U-boats: the Lusitania was sunk in May 1915 (1,198 dead, including 128 Americans). Unrestricted submarine warfare from February 1917 sank huge tonnage (Britain had only weeks of grain at one point) and helped bring the USA into the war. The convoy system (from mid-1917), depth charges, Q-ships and mines defeated the U-boat threat.',
                'Gallipoli: Churchill’s plan to knock out the Ottoman Empire and open a supply route to Russia. The naval attack on the Dardanelles failed (18 March 1915); landings at Cape Helles and ANZAC Cove (25 April 1915) and Suvla Bay (August) were pinned down by Ottoman defenders under Mustafa Kemal. Reasons for failure: poor planning, loss of surprise, weak leadership (Hamilton), difficult terrain, disease and strong Ottoman resistance. Evacuated December 1915-January 1916 with almost no losses; about 250,000 Allied casualties.',
                'Eastern Front: Russian defeats at Tannenberg (August 1914) and the Masurian Lakes (September 1914); the Brusilov Offensive (1916) was a major success but costly. Revolution in 1917 led to the Bolshevik Treaty of Brest-Litovsk (March 1918), in which Russia lost about a third of its population and much of its industry. The Eastern Front tied down German troops but its end let Germany move about a million men west for 1918.',
                'Civilians in Britain: DORA (Defence of the Realm Act, August 1914) allowed censorship and control of industry; conscription from January 1916; Zeppelin and Gotha bomber raids; German naval shelling of Scarborough, Hartlepool and Whitby (December 1914); women worked in munitions; food rationing from 1918.',
                'Civilians in Germany: the British blockade caused severe shortages - the "turnip winter" of 1916-17 - and hundreds of thousands of deaths from malnutrition and disease, undermining morale.',
