              title:'Features and types of volcanoes and earthquakes',
              points:[
                'Volcano features: magma chamber, main vent, secondary vent, crater, layers of ash and lava, pyroclastic flows, lahars (volcanic mudflows).',
                'Strato-volcano (composite cone): steep-sided, alternate layers of ash and viscous andesitic lava, explosive eruptions, at destructive boundaries (e.g. Mt Fuji, Mt Merapi).',
                'Shield volcano: broad, gentle slopes of runny basaltic lava that flows long distances; frequent but gentle eruptions; at constructive boundaries or hotspots (e.g. Mauna Loa, Hawaii).',
                'Earthquake features: focus (point underground where rock fractures and energy is released), epicentre (point on the surface directly above the focus), seismic waves spreading outward; shallow-focus quakes cause more damage.',
                'Magnitude is measured on the moment magnitude scale (replacing the Richter scale), which is logarithmic — each whole number is about 32 times more energy (10 times the ground shaking amplitude). Intensity/damage is described by the Mercalli scale.',
              ],
              workedExample:`Label-and-explain answer: formation of a strato-volcano (5 marks)
1. At a destructive boundary the oceanic plate is subducted beneath the continental plate.
2. Friction and heat at 100+ km depth melt the plate, forming magma.
3. The magma is less dense so rises through cracks into a magma chamber.
4. It is viscous and gas-rich, so pressure builds until it erupts explosively through the vent.
5. Alternate eruptions of ash and lava build up steep-sided layers around the crater.`,
