              points:[
                'Cash flow forecast: cash inflows – cash outflows = net cash flow; opening balance + net cash flow = closing balance (which becomes next month’s opening balance).',
                'Total cost = fixed costs + variable costs; variable cost = variable cost per unit × quantity.',
                'Contribution per unit = selling price – variable cost per unit.',
                'Break-even output = fixed costs ÷ contribution per unit. Margin of safety = actual output – break-even output.',
                'Income statement: revenue – cost of sales = gross profit; gross profit – expenses = profit for the year.',
                'Charities record a surplus (not profit); a negative result is a deficit/loss.',
                'Accurate records give a true and fair view to stakeholders, are needed for tax/legal reasons and support forecasting and decisions.',
              ],
              workedExample:`Break-even: a cupcake enterprise has fixed costs of $120 per month. Price = $2.50, variable cost = $1.00 per cake.
Contribution = 2.50 – 1.00 = $1.50 per cake
Break-even = 120 ÷ 1.50 = 80 cakes per month
If it sells 110 cakes: margin of safety = 110 – 80 = 30 cakes; profit = 30 × 1.50 = $45.

Cash flow (Month 1): opening balance $200, inflows $350, outflows $420
Net cash flow = 350 – 420 = –$70; closing balance = 200 – 70 = $130 (Month 2 opening).

Income statement: revenue $2,000, cost of sales $900, expenses $600
Gross profit = 2,000 – 900 = $1,100; profit = 1,100 – 600 = $500.`,
